WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.120
Today we are going to use this&nbsp;
new AI research work to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:17.400
absolutely magical videos almost out of&nbsp;
thin air. Like this one. And this one. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.040
with this new work, we can all become&nbsp;
movie directors. Sort of. Loving it.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:30.840
Now, everyone knows that modern AI techniques are&nbsp;
already capable of text to image, that is taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.500
piece of text and creating a picture out of it.
What fewer people know, but many of you Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.300
Scholars definitely know is that&nbsp;
text to video is now also possible.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:49.620
However, I bet that you didn’t know about&nbsp;
today’s paper! So, what is this about? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.140
whenever I use these systems, I always&nbsp;
feel myself yearning for more artistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.940
control. I want to specify the pose&nbsp;
for these characters. The framing too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.960
what should be visible, where the characters&nbsp;
and objects are. And more. That would be lovely.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.840
So, in this new paper scientists had&nbsp;
an excellent question - why not give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.200
more information to the AI for these videos?&nbsp;
And that is an excellent question. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.180
this new technique allows us to show&nbsp;
the AI exactly what we are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.640
for by performing it ourselves&nbsp;
and giving it that as an input.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:36.480
It goes the following way: imagine that we would&nbsp;
like to have a video of an astronaut surfing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:43.920
but obviously, that’s not us. So, first, we record&nbsp;
ourselves surfing, give this video to the AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:50.340
add a text prompt that now an astronaut&nbsp;
should be surfing, and, I am very excited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:57.360
let’s see. Oh yes, that is so cool! And, if&nbsp;
we wish to reimagine what this scene would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:05.280
look like if we were surfing in the desert, not a&nbsp;
problem. Or, we can also think bigger, and ask for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:13.260
a surfing sloth instead. Look at that. This sloth&nbsp;
is having the time of his life. And I particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:19.920
like this example because this almost feels like&nbsp;
a motion capture video where we just provide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.660
movement for some other character. And all this&nbsp;
with just one video and one text prompt. Wow.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:33.720
Now wait, this new work also gives us a&nbsp;
ton more tools to play with. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:41.280
if we have a video of a bear playing a guitar, a&nbsp;
common occurrence, we can also ask it to make it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:49.080
little more artistic. And if we have a cat, we can&nbsp;
make it two, add cowboy hats, or even remove that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:56.940
hat and have it synthesize a similar video. I also&nbsp;
loved this example. A simple video of this good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.600
boy running in the garden, and we can use it as&nbsp;
the blueprint for our text to image ideas. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.540
it has been changed to a corgi and it is&nbsp;
now in a different environment, or a lion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:16.860
or whatever we can imagine. So I hear you asking&nbsp;
- what is so cool about this example? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.860
imagine if we are very picky with the kind of&nbsp;
footage we want to synthesize, we would have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:30.060
write in text what the pace, direction and the&nbsp;
exact angle should the walking take place in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:37.980
But this paper says, none of that is required,&nbsp;
just show the AI what you wish to see. So good.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.980
Now, this is not the first paper to attempt&nbsp;
this, so let’s see what previous methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.200
were made of. Oh boy. Look. This is&nbsp;
the Video Diffusion Models paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.700
VDM in short, and the quality of&nbsp;
the outputs is not the greatest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.700 --> 00:04:01.800
but there is an even bigger problem. What is the&nbsp;
problem? Well, temporal coherence. Each adjacent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.840
frame seems so different, for instance,&nbsp;
the color of the clothing is different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:14.700
each time. If we weave these together into a&nbsp;
video, we will get a ton of flickering. Not good.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:22.740
So, let’s see the new method. Whoa. This&nbsp;
is so much better. Let’s do another one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.660
these fun comparisons. Let’s&nbsp;
see….yes this is CogVideo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:34.800
another earlier technique. The prompt is “a cat&nbsp;
running on a single-plank bridge, comic style”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:43.260
Wow, I don’t know about this one. Look. I&nbsp;
am not sure if this is a cat, not sure if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:50.520
this is a bridge, and I am not sure if this is&nbsp;
in a comic style. The rest is fine though. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.880
let’s see the new one. Wow. This is&nbsp;
incredible improvement in just…wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:04.260
actually, in how long exactly? When were these&nbsp;
two previous works published? Is this new work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:11.880
10 years of progress? No-no, not at all. And this&nbsp;
is the part where I fell of the chair when reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:18.000
this paper. Hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
both of these previous works were published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:26.340
less then a year before this new one. My goodness.&nbsp;
That is amazing progress in just a few months.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:32.400
Now, we are experienced Fellow Scholars over&nbsp;
here, so we are also looking for a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:39.660
details regarding the old vs new technique.&nbsp;
So let’s dig a little deeper…and, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:47.100
a human evaluation on a set of 32 videos,&nbsp;
and are you seeing what I am seeing? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:52.500
is one of the biggest improvements in just&nbsp;
a few months that I have ever seen. Almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.500 --> 00:06:00.060
everyone favored the new one. Bravo! And just&nbsp;
imagine what we will be able to do just a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:06.840
more papers down the line. Who knows, we all might&nbsp;
become movie directors. What a time to be alive!

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:12.780
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? Let me know in the comments below!

